<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Logic Notation and Truth Tables</title>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Vocabulary and Notation</title>

            <tabular>
                <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
                <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
                <col halign="left" width="60%"/>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Word</cell>
                    <cell>Symbol</cell>
                    <cell>Meaning</cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Statement</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell>
                        <line>
                        A <em>statement</em> is a sentence or mathematical expression 
                        </line>
                        <line> that is true or false. </line>
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Open Sentence</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell>
                        <line>    An <em>open sentence</em> is a sentence or mathematical
                        expression </line>
                        <line> which does not have a truth value, depends on
                        some unknown, </line>
                        <line> becomes a statement  when the unknown is
                        specified. </line>
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>And</cell>
                    <cell><m>\wedge</m></cell>
                    <cell>
                        <m>P\wedge Q</m> is true when both <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m>
                        are true.
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Or</cell>
                    <cell><m>\vee</m></cell>
                    <cell>
                        <m>P \vee Q</m> is true when at least one of <m>P</m> or
                        <m>Q</m> is true.
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Not (or Negation)</cell>
                    <cell><m>\sim</m></cell>
                    <cell>
                        <m> \neg P </m> has the opposite truth value of
                        <m>P </m>
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>
                        <line> Conditional Statement </line>
                        <line> <q>If...then</q> </line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell><m>\implies</m></cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line> <m>P\implies Q</m> means <q> If <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m> </q> and  is only false 
                      </line>
                        <line> in situations where <m>P</m> is true and <m>Q</m> is false. </line>
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Hypothesis</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell>
                        In a conditional statement
                        <q> If <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m> </q>, <m>P</m> is the
                        hypothesis.
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Conclusion</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell>
                        In a conditional statement
                        <q> If <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m> </q>, <m>Q</m> is the
                        conclusion.
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>
                        <line> Biconditional Statement </line>
                        <line> <q> if and only if</q>, or <q> iff </q> </line>
                    </cell>
                    <cell><m>\Leftrightarrow</m></cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line> A biconditional statement is of the form <m>P</m> if and
                    only if <m>Q</m>. </line>
                        <line> It means <m>P\implies Q</m> and <m>Q\implies P</m>. </line>
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Converse</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell>
                        The converse of <m>P\implies Q</m> is
                        <m>Q\implies P</m>.
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Logically Equivalent</cell>
                    <cell><m>\Leftrightarrow</m></cell>
                    <cell>
                        <line> A statement is <em>logically equivalent</em> to another
                    statement if they</line>
                        <line>  have the same truth value on all possible
                    inputs. </line>
                        <line> E.g.,
                    <m>\sim (P \wedge Q) \Leftrightarrow \sim P \,\vee \sim Q</m>
                    by DeMorgan's Laws </line>
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>For all</cell>
                    <cell><m>\forall</m></cell>
                    <cell>Also, ``For ever", ``For each"</cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>There exists</cell>
                    <cell><m>\exists</m></cell>
                    <cell>Also ``For some"</cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Quantifier</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell>
                        <line> Indicates the number or quantity of our objects. </line>
                        <line>  <m>\forall</m> is the universal quantifier and</line>
                        <line> <m>\exists</m> is the existential quantifier </line>
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Tautology</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell>
                        A statement that is true no matter what, e.g.,
                        <m>S\vee \sim S</m>
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Contradiction</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell>
                        A statement that is never true, e.g.,
                        <m>S\wedge \sim S</m>
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Converse</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell>
                        The converse of <m>P\implies Q</m> is
                        <m>Q\implies P</m>.
                    </cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Contrapositive</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell>
                        The contrapositive of <m>P\implies Q</m> is
                        <m>\sim Q \implies \sim P</m>.
                    </cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Truth Tables</title>

            <p>
                We use truth tables to organize information about the truth
                value of complicated statements based on the truth values of the
                pieces of that statement.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>

        <activity>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Fill in the following truth table.
                    </p>

                    <table>
                        <title>Tables for <q>And</q>, <q>Or</q></title>

                        <tabular halign="center">
                            <col right="minor"/>
                            <col right="minor"/>
                            <col right="minor"/>
                            <col/>
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell><m>P</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>Q</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>P\wedge Q</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>P\vee Q</m></cell>
                            </row>

                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell>True</cell>
                                <cell>True</cell>
                                <cell/>
                                <cell/>
                            </row>

                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell>True</cell>
                                <cell/>
                                <cell/>
                                <cell/>
                            </row>

                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell>False</cell>
                                <cell/>
                                <cell/>
                                <cell/>
                            </row>

                            <row>
                                <cell>False</cell>
                                <cell/>
                                <cell/>
                                <cell/>
                            </row>
                        </tabular>
                    </table>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Fill in the following truth table.
                    </p>

                    <table>
                        <title>Table for <q>Not</q></title>

                        <tabular halign="center">
                            <col right="minor"/>
                            <col/>
                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell><m>P</m></cell>
                                <cell><m>\neg P\,\,\,\,</m></cell>
                            </row>

                            <row bottom="minor">
                                <cell>True</cell>
                                <cell/>
                            </row>

                            <row>
                                <cell>False</cell>
                                <cell/>
                            </row>
                        </tabular>
                    </table>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Suppose <m>P</m> is the statement
                        <q> <m>15</m> is odd</q> and <m>Q</m> is the statement
                        <q> <m>15</m> is prime</q>.
                        What are some true statements we can form using
                        <m>P, Q, \wedge, \vee, \sim</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </activity>

     


    </page>
</handout>